Q: How can I more conveniently see the code mentioned in the stack backtrace in the Perl debugger in Emacs? A: <p>The best I've found so far is to modify the stack backtrace format so it becomes compatible with <code>compilation-mode</code>, and then use functionality from that mode for the navigation.  Are there better ways?</p>
<p>To modify the stack backtrace format, add the following to <code>sub afterinit</code> in the perl debugger initialization file (<em>perldb.ini</em> on Windows, <em>.perldb</em> on other operating systems, see <a href="https://perldoc.perl.org/perldebug" rel="nofollow noreferrer">perldb</a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>  my $old_print_trace = \&amp;DB::print_trace;
  *{DB::print_trace} = sub {
    my $trace = '';
    open my $fh, '&gt;', \$trace;
    $old_print_trace-&gt;($fh, 1); # write old trace to $trace
    close $fh;
    # transform trace to compilation-mode format
    my @lines = split /\n/, $trace;
    # this override introduces one extra stack frame; omit it from the
    # trace
    shift @lines;
    my $ix = 0;
    foreach (@lines) {
      ++$ix;
      s/^(.*?) called from file '(.*?)' line (\d+)/$2:$3:[$ix]$1/;
    }
    print join(&quot;\n&quot;, @lines), &quot;\n&quot;;
  }
</code></pre>
<p>This code overrides <code>DB::print_trace</code> (called by the debugger <code>T</code> command) to reformat the trace before printing.  Don't forget to make the perldb file read-only (again) after you've modified it, otherwise perl won't accept it.</p>
<p>Then starting the debugger and eventually running the <code>T</code> command produces a stack backtrace similar to this:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Ambulation.pm:10:[1]$ = main::infested
camel_flea:7:[2]@ = Ambulation::legs(1, 2, 3, 4)
camel_flea:4[3]$ = main::pests('bactrian', 4) called from file 'camel_flea' line 4
</code></pre>
<p>The bracketed numbers (like <code>[1]</code>) just after the file name and line number identify the number of the stack frame, for use with the debugger's <code>y</code> command.  Then running <code>M-x compilation-minor-mode</code> turns the filename/line parts of the backtrace into clickable links.  Click on the desired one to open the relevant file/line in a separate buffer.  Then <code>C-x `</code> navigates to the next frame of the backtrace just like when navigating the errors of a compilation.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, no debugger commands can be run as long as compilation-minor-mode is active.  Run <code>M-x compilation-minor-mode</code> again to turn off the minor mode.  Then debugger commands can be entered again.</p>

